    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>In the <strong>under desk elliptical vs bike</strong> debate, the main difference is the path your feet travel. An under desk elliptical moves your feet in a long, flat oval, like a full-size elliptical trainer cut in half. An under desk bike, also called a pedal exerciser or desk cycle, spins your feet in a circle, like a stationary bike without the seat.</p>
<p>That one difference drives almost everything else: how high your knees rise, whether the machine fits under your desk, how it feels on your joints and how hard a workout you can get. For most people working at a standard desk, the elliptical is the easier daily habit. A bike is the better pick if you want more resistance and a desk with plenty of room.</p>

<h2>Under Desk Elliptical vs Bike: Quick Comparison</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Under desk elliptical</th><th>Under desk bike</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Foot path</td><td>Flat oval glide</td><td>Full circle</td></tr>
<tr><td>Knee lift</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher at the top of each turn</td></tr>
<tr><td>Desk clearance</td><td>Fits more desks</td><td>Needs more room under the desk</td></tr>
<tr><td>Joint feel</td><td>Smooth, very low stress</td><td>Low impact but more knee bend</td></tr>
<tr><td>Resistance</td><td>Light to moderate on most models</td><td>Often a wider, higher range</td></tr>
<tr><td>Muscles</td><td>Quads, hamstrings, glutes, calves, with more hamstring and glute involvement</td><td>Quad-dominant, plus calves and glutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typing while pedaling</td><td>Very easy</td><td>Easy at low resistance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Noise</td><td>Near silent on magnetic models</td><td>Quiet on magnetic models</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>The Motion: Glide vs Spin</h2>
<p>On an elliptical, your foot stays flat on a pedal that slides forward and back while rising slightly. Your knee bends only a little, and the stride feels a lot like walking in place while sitting. Many owners say they stop noticing it within a few minutes, which is exactly what you want for something you use during work.</p>
<p>On a bike, your foot pushes down and around a crank. At the top of each turn your knee bends sharply and rises toward the underside of the desk. The motion is familiar to anyone who has ridden a bicycle, and it lets you push harder against resistance, which is why it can feel more like exercise.</p>
<h3>Which muscles each one works</h3>
<p>Both machines work the same main muscle groups: quadriceps, hamstrings, glutes and calves. The bike leans harder on the quads because most of the effort comes from pushing down. The elliptical's longer, flatter stride spreads the work more evenly, and pushing back through the heel brings in more hamstring and glute. Neither one trains your upper body or core in a meaningful way. For a full breakdown, read <a href="/what-muscles-does-an-under-desk-elliptical-work/">what muscles an under desk elliptical works</a>.</p>
<h3>What this means for your knees</h3>
<p>If you have stiff or sore knees, the elliptical's smaller knee bend is usually more comfortable. The bike's deeper bend can bother some people over long sessions, though others find it fine at low resistance. You can read more about <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-benefits/">the benefits of an under desk elliptical</a> for joints and circulation. If you have a knee or hip condition, ask your doctor or physical therapist which motion suits you before you start.</p>

<h2>Desk Clearance: The Deciding Factor for Many People</h2>
<p>Knee clearance is the single biggest reason people send back under desk machines. Because a bike lifts your knees higher, it needs more vertical space between your seat and the desk. With a standard desk and an average chair, taller users often bump the underside or a keyboard tray.</p>
<p>An under desk elliptical keeps your knees lower, so it clears more desks without changes. Low-profile models such as the [[amazon:deskcycle-ellipse]] are designed with this in mind. If you prefer a bike, look for one with a low pedal height like the [[amazon:deskcycle-2|DeskCycle 2]], lower your chair a notch or sit further back.</p>
<p>Before buying either, check your space with our <a href="/tools/desk-clearance-checker/">desk clearance checker</a>.</p>
[[tool:clearance]]

<h2>Calories and Workout Intensity</h2>
<p>For calorie burn, the gap between the two is smaller than marketing suggests. Seated pedaling of any kind falls around 2.0 METs at light effort and about 2.8 METs at moderate effort, compared with roughly 1.3 for sitting still. The real difference is ceiling: bikes often have more resistance on tap, so if you want to push hard during a lunch break, a bike lets you go further.</p>
<p>During actual work, most people keep resistance low on either machine so they can type and think. At that level, a 160 lb (73 kg) person burns roughly 150 to 185 calories an hour, depending on effort. Over two or three hours of pedaling a day, that adds up. Our <a href="/does-under-desk-elliptical-burn-calories/">calorie guide</a> explains the math in more detail.</p>
<h3>Are under the desk bikes effective?</h3>
<p>Yes, for what they are designed to do: break up sitting and add low-level activity across your day. This kind of everyday movement is what researchers call NEAT (non-exercise activity thermogenesis), an area the Mayo Clinic has studied for years. Neither a desk bike nor an elliptical will replace a run or a gym session, but both may help with circulation, stiffness and energy levels, and they can move you toward the 150 minutes of moderate activity a week recommended by the CDC and WHO when combined with other exercise.</p>

<h2>Noise, Stability and Everyday Use</h2>
<p>Magnetic resistance makes both types quiet enough for calls. Cheaper friction-based models, whether bike or elliptical, tend to get louder over time. If you share an office, our <a href="/quietest-under-desk-elliptical/">quietest under desk elliptical</a> roundup focuses on models that stay quiet.</p>
<p>Both types can slide away from you on hard floors, especially when you push harder. A rubber mat under the machine and caster cups under your chair solve that for either one. Bikes, with their stronger push at the bottom of each stroke, tend to creep more.</p>
<h3>What about a full desk bike?</h3>
<p>There is a third option: a bike with its own built-in desk, such as the [[amazon:fitdesk-fdx2|FitDesk FDX 2.0]]. It is not an under desk machine. You sit on its saddle and work on its small desk surface. It gives a more serious seated cardio workout, but you give up your normal chair and desk. Read our <a href="/fitdesk-fdx-2-review/">FitDesk FDX 2.0 review</a> if that appeals to you.</p>

<h2>Under Desk Bike vs Elliptical: What Owners Report</h2>
<p>If you have read owner threads on Reddit or in product reviews, the same themes come up again and again. They are worth knowing before you buy, because they are the problems that show up after the first month rather than on day one.</p>
<h3>Common feedback on under desk bikes</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Knees hitting the desk</strong> is the top complaint, especially from taller users and anyone with a keyboard tray.</li>
<li><strong>Sliding forward</strong> on hard floors when resistance is turned up. A mat fixes most of it.</li>
<li><strong>A better "real workout" feel</strong> than an elliptical, which owners who use it for short, hard sessions appreciate.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Common feedback on under desk ellipticals</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Easy to forget you are moving</strong>, which leads to longer sessions during work.</li>
<li><strong>Resistance can feel light</strong> for fit users who want to sweat.</li>
<li><strong>Squeaks on budget models</strong> after a few weeks, which is why brand choice matters more here.</li>
</ul>
<p>The pattern is clear: people who want exercise lean toward bikes, and people who want movement while they work lean toward ellipticals. Decide which of those you are before you shop.</p>

<h2>Setup Tips for Either Machine</h2>
<p>Whichever side of the under desk elliptical vs bike question you land on, the setup is similar and it makes a big difference to how often you use it.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Use a chair that stays put.</strong> A chair without wheels is ideal. If yours rolls, add caster cups.</li>
<li><strong>Put a mat under the machine.</strong> It stops creeping, protects the floor and damps vibration.</li>
<li><strong>Find your distance.</strong> Your knee should stay slightly bent at the furthest point of the stroke. Too close and your knees rise into the desk; too far and you slouch.</li>
<li><strong>Start with light resistance.</strong> Keep it low for typing and turn it up for calls or reading.</li>
<li><strong>Pedal in blocks.</strong> Ten to fifteen minutes at a time is easier to sustain than one long session. Our <a href="/tools/desk-pedal-timer/">desk pedal timer</a> can prompt you.</li>
</ol>

